1
00:00:01,130 --> 00:00:04,770
Here's a timeline we built
of George Washington's life,

2
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:12,520
from his birth in Virginia in 1732 to
his death at Mount Vernon in 1799.

3
00:00:12,570 --> 00:00:16,610
His homes, commands,
offices and wars are bands;

4
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:20,820
everything else is a moment,
with the sources it draws on.

5
00:00:20,870 --> 00:00:23,314
Chapter headings mark
the stages of his life.

6
00:00:24,864 --> 00:00:28,314
Open his birth, and the date needs a note.

7
00:00:28,364 --> 00:00:32,814
Virginia was British, and Britain
still used the old Julian calendar,

8
00:00:32,864 --> 00:00:37,644
with the year beginning in March. So
the day was written as the eleventh of

9
00:00:37,694 --> 00:00:43,664
February, 1731. The timeline
puts it on today's calendar,

10
00:00:43,714 --> 00:00:48,764
the twenty-second of February 1732,
and keeps the original beside it,

11
00:00:48,814 --> 00:00:54,988
linked to the day in 1752 when Britain
switched and eleven days vanished.

12
00:00:56,538 --> 00:01:01,298
Zoom into his youth: the move to Ferry
Farm, his father's death when he was

13
00:01:01,348 --> 00:01:05,698
eleven, his first surveying trip
over the Blue Ridge at sixteen,

14
00:01:05,748 --> 00:01:08,968
the voyage to Barbados
with his brother Lawrence,

15
00:01:09,018 --> 00:01:13,038
where George caught smallpox,
and Lawrence's death in 1752,

16
00:01:13,088 --> 00:01:16,142
which in the end brought him Mount Vernon.

17
00:01:17,582 --> 00:01:23,682
At twenty-one he carried Virginia's
warning to the French in the Ohio Country.

18
00:01:23,732 --> 00:01:28,532
The next spring came Jumonville Glen, a
skirmish that helped start a world war,

19
00:01:28,582 --> 00:01:32,862
then surrender at Fort Necessity,
Braddock's defeat on the Monongahela,

20
00:01:32,912 --> 00:01:38,892
and command of the Virginia Regiment
until Fort Duquesne fell in 1758.

21
00:01:40,612 --> 00:01:45,062
Then the longest band of his career:
eight and a half years in command of the

22
00:01:45,112 --> 00:01:49,602
Continental Army. Inside
it, the siege of Boston,

23
00:01:49,652 --> 00:01:52,942
the loss of New York, and
the Ten Crucial Days:

24
00:01:52,992 --> 00:01:55,632
the crossing of the Delaware
on Christmas night,

25
00:01:55,682 --> 00:01:59,852
Trenton the next morning,
Princeton eight days later.

26
00:01:59,902 --> 00:02:03,816
Then Valley Forge, Monmouth,
and the surrender at Yorktown.

27
00:02:05,366 --> 00:02:10,706
And the band ends by choice. At Newburgh
he talked down officers ready to defy

28
00:02:10,756 --> 00:02:16,056
Congress, and at Annapolis, two
days before Christmas 1783,

29
00:02:16,106 --> 00:02:18,921
he handed back his
commission and went home.

30
00:02:20,651 --> 00:02:24,751
Within four years he was back, presiding
over the convention that wrote the

31
00:02:24,801 --> 00:02:31,121
Constitution, and in 1789 he
became the first president.

32
00:02:31,171 --> 00:02:34,831
Two inaugurations, the first
cabinet, Hamilton's Bank,

33
00:02:34,881 --> 00:02:37,381
neutrality in the war
between Britain and France,

34
00:02:37,431 --> 00:02:41,141
the Whiskey Rebellion, when he
rode out with the militia himself,

35
00:02:41,191 --> 00:02:46,057
the Jay Treaty, and a Farewell Address
that turned down a third term.

36
00:02:47,767 --> 00:02:52,637
Now pull out one thread. Separate
by tags, choose slavery,

37
00:02:52,687 --> 00:02:56,207
and the people Mount Vernon ran on
rise into a track of their own:

38
00:02:56,257 --> 00:02:59,467
the ten people he inherited
at eleven, William Lee,

39
00:02:59,517 --> 00:03:02,467
who rode beside him through
the whole war, Ona Judge,

40
00:03:02,517 --> 00:03:06,467
who escaped from the
President's House in 1796,

41
00:03:06,517 --> 00:03:12,000
and Hercules, the cook, who escaped
on Washington's sixty-fifth birthday.

42
00:03:13,610 --> 00:03:18,050
His will freed the people he
owned, after Martha's death.

43
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:22,380
Martha freed them sooner,
on New Year's Day 1801.

44
00:03:22,430 --> 00:03:26,360
The hundred and fifty-three held through
her first husband's estate were not

45
00:03:26,410 --> 00:03:29,552
theirs to free, and stayed enslaved.

46
00:03:31,012 --> 00:03:36,902
He died at Mount Vernon in December
1799, two days after a ride in the snow,

47
00:03:36,952 --> 00:03:42,402
and every band of his life ends there.
It's a timeline we built from Wikipedia,

48
00:03:42,452 --> 00:03:45,672
his papers and Mount Vernon's
own research: open it,

49
00:03:45,722 --> 00:03:48,986
follow the sources, and keep going.
